Copyright: 2025-2026 Çağan ERGÜN <73942784+caganerg@users.noreply.github.com>
           2006-2008 Aurélien Croc (AP²C) <aurelien@ap2c.org>
License: GPL-2
 These files carry material transcribed from OpenPrinting SpliX
 (https://github.com/OpenPrinting/splix), which is GPL-2.0-only. They are
 therefore a derived work and this package cannot be relicensed as GPL-2+.
 .
 The list changed shape in the 2.0 line without changing what is derived: the
 engine moved out of the binary into the spl2-core crate, and at gate P11 the
 1.x filter was deleted while its page loop stayed. What this paragraph used to
 call src/spl.rs is now crates/spl2-core/src/qpdl.rs, the geometry rules that
 used to sit in src/main.rs are now split between
 crates/spl2-core/src/geometry.rs and crates/spl2-core/src/engine.rs, and what
 src/main.rs held last is crates/spl2-core/src/replay.rs. The files moved; the
 transcription did not grow.
 .
 crates/spl2-core/src/qpdl.rs states the derivation in its own header: the
 QPDL wire constants, the PJL job header and its exact line order, the 17-byte
 page header layout and the Algo 0x11 RLE band encoding all come from SpliX's
 qpdl.cpp, compress.cpp, algo0x11.cpp and printer.cpp.
 .
 crates/spl2-core/src/geometry.rs and crates/spl2-core/src/engine.rs
 transcribe SpliX's geometry rules, each cited at the function that implements
 it: the page-width computation from document.cpp, the hard margin and
 column-major band fill from compress.cpp _compressBandedPage, the duplex
 mapping from request.cpp, and the "halve the band height at 300x300" rule
 from compress.cpp.
 .
 crates/spl2-core/src/media.rs holds one derived constant: the 12.5 pt hard
 margin, which follows SpliX's ML-2165 override.
 .
 crates/spl2-core/src/replay.rs is the 1.x filter's page loop, which outlived
 the filter itself at gate P11. It delegates the protocol and the geometry to
 the rest of spl2-core and keeps the SpliX citations at the call sites that
 explain the byte-for-byte behaviour — the band stride and the reason
 cupsCompression is ignored. It is behind the non-default `golden-replay`
 feature, so the shipping printer application does not contain it.
 .
 ppd/samsung-ml2160.ppd takes its QPDL paper-source codes, media-type
 keywords and band size from the sibling PPDs upstream SpliX ships for this
 engine family (ml1910, ml2010, ml2525, ml1640, ml2510), as its own comments
 record. It is not installed by this package and is kept permanently as project
 data (Q-5); its *cupsFilter lines still name rastertospl-rust, the 1.x binary
 deleted at P11, because they describe the queue that binary served.
 .
 crates/spl2-core/src/raster.rs and the two files under
 crates/spl2-core/tests/ are NOT derived from SpliX: raster.rs implements the
 CUPS Raster container format, and golden.rs and filter.rs are this project's
 own test harness and the 1.x filter's own test module, kept after P11. Each mentions SpliX once, as a cross-reference
 rather than as transcribed material.
Comment: The upstream notice was transcribed from the SpliX 2.0.1 source
 (Debian source package splix 2.0.1-1, from http://splix.ap2c.org/). Every
 file this project draws from — qpdl.cpp, compress.cpp, algo0x11.cpp,
 document.cpp, printer.cpp and request.cpp — carries the same header line,
 "(C) 2006-2008, Aurélien Croc (AP²C)", and the package's own debian/copyright
 names Aurélien Croc <aurelien@ap2c.org> as the upstream author under GPL
 version 2 only. The PPD values come from ppd/samsung.drv.in and the defs it
 imports, headed "(C) 2007, Aurélien Croc (AP²C)", which the range above
 covers.

Files: crates/pappl-sys/*
       crates/pappl/*
Copyright: 2026 Çağan ERGÜN
License: MIT
 The two FFI crates are permissively licensed so that they stay reusable
 outside this project while remaining linkable into the GPL-2.0-only binary:
 MIT carries none of the "further restrictions" that GPLv2 section 6 forbids,
 which Apache-2.0's patent-termination and notice clauses would. They were
 dual-licensed "Apache-2.0 OR MIT" until 2026-09-10; the Apache-2.0 arm was
 dropped because a consumer could always elect MIT anyway, so it added a
 second licence text without adding a guarantee. See docs/DECISIONS.md, Q-8a.

